  "text": "Let's start by determining the required ratio for each kind of food item across all groups. \n\nAthlete boxes contain 3 chicken, 2 vegetarian, and 3 salads. Coach boxes contain 2 chicken, 1 vegetarian, and 2 salads. Individual packages for officials can be 1 chicken, 1 vegetarian, or 1 salad. \n\nTo ensure they can supply the same variety of food items for each group, we need to find LCMs of these quantities to get whole numbers for each group.\n\nLCM of chicken ingredients for athletes (3), coaches (2), and officials (1) is 6.\nLCM of vegetarian ingredients for athletes (2), coaches (1), and officials (1) is 2.\nLCM of salads ingredients for athletes (3), coaches (2), and officials (1) is 6.\n\nNow, let's find out how many larger boxes (athletes, coaches) and smaller boxes (coaches) are needed to meet these requirements:\n\nAthlers: 6 chicken / 3 chicken per box * 2 cookies per box = 4 boxes\nAthlers: 6 vegetarian / 2 vegetarian per box * 1 cookie per box = 3 boxes\nAthlers: 6 salads / 3 salads per box = 2 boxes\n\nCoaches: 6 chicken / 2 chicken per box = 3 boxes\nCoaches: 2 vegetarian / 1 vegetarian per box * 1 cookie per box = 2 boxes\nCoaches: 6 salads / 2 salads per box = 3 boxes\n\nOfficials: They need 2 more chicken sandwiches, 1 more vegetarian sandwich, and 1 more salad. \nSince each official package can provide 1 of each, no additional package is needed for the officials.\n\nThus, we need 2 large boxes (for athletes) and 2 smaller boxes and 4 individual packages to meet the requirements.\n\nTotal meals = 2+2+4 = 8 meals.\n\nSo, the least possible total number of meals they need to prepare is \\boxed{8}.",
